  <p class="energy-kicker">Sources and notes</p><h2 id="energy-sources-heading">
    What these figures measure
  </h2>
  <p>
    The state pump-price comparison uses AAA’s September 19, 2026 daily averages
    for regular gasoline and diesel. EIA’s September 14 weekly prices appear
    separately in the essay. The two sources use different observation dates and
    methods; they are not combined into a single series.
  </p>
  <p>
    Residential electricity prices are EIA’s preliminary June 2026 averages,
    released August 26. They divide revenue by kilowatt-hours sold in the
    residential sector. They are not quoted tariffs or typical household bills.
    BLS inflation figures describe the 13-state West’s urban consumers and
    measure changes from August 2025 to August 2026.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fuel-budget calculation multiplies monthly gallons by a selected price
    change and holds use constant for two months. It does not estimate how
    driving, wages or other prices respond. The New Mexico illustration
    separately applies the August 2026 forecast’s $56.45 million annual
    direct-revenue sensitivity per $1-per-barrel price change, holding other
    assumptions constant. It is not a prediction of cash receipts, a household
    rebate or a net-benefit estimate.
  </p>
  <p>
    The supply diagram and step relationships show economic connections, not proportional flows. The
    outlook cases are conditional descriptions, without assigned probabilities
    or price bands. Research is current through September 19, 2026; EIA’s
    September outlook used assumptions finalized on September 3. Later market
    news is identified separately in the essay.
  </p>
